window.docContents[146].resolve({"/IEC-80000-6-___-Electromagnetism/The-AC-power-family___-distinguishing-kinds-by-defining-construction/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--IEC-80000-6-___-Electromagnetism--The-AC-power-family___-distinguishing-kinds-by-defining-construction":{"contents":"IEC 80000-6 lists power (6-45) as a genus and the AC power quantities — active power\n(6-56), apparent power (6-57), complex power (6-59), reactive power (6-60), and non-active\npower (6-61) — as items of one dimension M·L²·T⁻³. The standard distinguishes them by\nwhich component of the periodic process each isolates: active power is the time-averaged\nreal (resistive) component; reactive power the imaginary part of the complex power;\napparent power the product of RMS voltage and current; and so on. Tellingly, it spends\nthree different unit strings on this single dimension — the watt for active power, the\nvar for reactive power, the volt-ampere for apparent power — precisely to keep the kinds\napart in print. A dimension-only model sees one type, \"a real number of watts\", for all\nof them.\n\nThis is requirement R2 again, now on the AC power quantities — the pattern Part 3 set on\nthe length family, Part 4 on the force family, and Part 5 on the thermodynamic potentials.\nItem 6-45 is the broad genus; each AC quantity is a species of power, distinguished not\nby fiat but by an explicit defining construction (Dybkær's examination principle, §7.5).\nThe species are then provably distinct kinds while remaining mutually comparable as\npowers — comparable enough to combine into the complex power P + jQ.\n\n\n\nA power species is a ratio-scale quantity-kind of dimension\nM·L²·T⁻³ carrying an examination principle — this work's own\nterse descriptor of the defining construction that distinguishes it (the time-averaged\nreal part, the RMS product, the complex sum P + jQ, the imaginary part, the residual\n√(S² − P²)), never the standard's normative definition. Active, apparent, complex,\nreactive, and non-active power are all species of the general power kind (item 6-45).\n\npowerSpecies id p := { kind := { id, scale := .ratio, examPrinciple := some p.id },\ndim := EDim.power }. The general power carries no examination principle; the\ndirect-parent edges of the family are collected in an Edge relation, each species an edge\nto power (mirroring the standard's listing of the AC quantities beneath power).\n\nActive and reactive power are distinct kinds — by defining construction, not by fiat.\nThey share dimension M·L²·T⁻³ and are both \"powers\", yet are different kinds because\nthey isolate different components of the periodic process (the time-averaged real part vs\nthe imaginary part of the complex power), proved through distinct_of_examPrinciple (§7.5).\nThis is the AC-power analogue of width ≠ distance, static ≠ kinetic friction force, and\nHelmholtz ≠ Gibbs energy. Uses the examination principle.\n\nactivePower_ne_reactivePower : activePower.kind ≠ reactivePower.kind, from\nKindOfProperty.distinct_of_examPrinciple applied to the differing principles\ntime-averaged-real and reactive-imaginary (a decide on the Option String links).\nThe companion apparentPower_ne_complexPower separates two further species, and\nactivePower_examinedBy checks the kind-to-principle link. Axiom-free.\n\nComparability is preserved. Active and reactive power, though distinct kinds, remain\nmutually comparable — they share the super-kind power, so combining them (into the\ncomplex power) is possible but only via an explicit up-cast, never silently. The companion\nactivePower_specializes_power records that active power specializes power. Uses\nspecialization.\n\nactivePower_reactivePower_comparable : MutuallyComparable Edge activePower.kind\nreactivePower.kind, with witness power.kind, each species reaching it by one edge.\nAxiom-free.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009IEC 80000-6 — Electromagnetism","header":"24.3. The AC power family: distinguishing kinds by defining construction","id":"/IEC-80000-6-___-Electromagnetism/The-AC-power-family___-distinguishing-kinds-by-defining-construction/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--IEC-80000-6-___-Electromagnetism--The-AC-power-family___-distinguishing-kinds-by-defining-construction"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too--Lesson":{"contents":"1. Open the box and represent configurations. Each body's upstream harmonic oscillator\nsupplies its wall-spring parameters; the coupling spring supplies stiffness, and each spring\nhas a signed extension. Two CoupledPair .P configurations admit a typed difference of\ntheir kC extensions:\n\n\n\nMass is now indexed by a body part and stiffness by a spring part. The oscillator-indexed\nL1 ω² was the frequency of an isolated oscillator; it is not the normal-mode frequency\nof this coupled pair.\n\n2. Assemble the pair's mass. The two body masses are parts of Composite.whole; rfl\nchecks that the total reads the upstream oscillator fields:\n\n\n\n3. Refuse whole plus part, and one pair plus another. Total mass is meaningful, but its\nwhole is not body A. A second pair is another system even when all parameters and part\nnames match, because PairId is nominal:\n\n\n\n4. Refuse to assemble angular frequency. The pair's license table has no entry for it:\n\n\n\n5. Give the normal mode its whole. A dedicated angular-frequency kind at pairS is\ndistinct from one dedicated to a body or a spring:\n\n\n\n6. Assemble parallel stiffness. With B clamped, A is held by kA and kC in parallel:\n\n\n\n7. Refuse series stiffness. For kA and kB chained end to end, no additive series license\nis registered:\n\n\n\n8. Check the licenses against measurements. The measurements use HCP's own Spring.\nPropertyValue.numeral is fixed at Int, so the exhibited kA–kB series chain is exact\n(6 ∥ 3 = 2); these integer numerals do not model longer chains:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe measurements verify the curation: parallel stiffness is extensive, while series\nstiffness is whole-proper. Measurement carries an Int numeral, so the exhibited\nkA–kB series value is exact (6 ∥ 3 = 2); these integer numerals do not model longer chains.\nRegistering the series sum would produce a magnitude that disagrees with its measurement.\n\nThe System-context card section of this chapter lists the bodies, all three springs,\nthe fixed walls, and the line of motion. The Lean model's nominal PartOf π index keeps\nparts of differently identified pairs distinct even when their parameters match, while\nbody mass, spring stiffness, and the pair's normal-mode frequency stay on their respective\nbearers. The on-axis, rigid, homogeneous, point-like body assumption returns at L3.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L2 — The pair is a system too","header":"5.3.7. Lesson","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too--Lesson"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L6-___-What-must-hold/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L6-___-What-must-hold--Lesson":{"contents":"1. Pair mass. R1 quantifies over every object of pairS. Its pair mass is assembled from\nthe body masses indexed to the named pair, and a witness theorem plus an executable check\nshows 3 + 5 = 8:\n\n\n\nThe body masses remain individual quantities on their body-in-pair bearers. Only pair\nmass has the Assembles pairS massK license:\n\n\n\n\n\n2. Whole-only frequency. R2 is provable by absence: the pair sort has no assembly\nlicense for the normal-mode kind. The input and output of the boundary remain attached to\nthe pair whole; the absence rules out summing body frequencies:\n\n\n\n3. Empirical range. R3 is scoped to the nominal object P. Under the one-name-one-system\nrule, it governs every state or record of P; the predicate is defined for every P record,\nand two Bool spot checks run it against the spring's datasheet limit. The gate mentions the\nreferent, so the requirement is discharged rather than attested:\n\n\n\nThe two failed scopes are counterexamples, so the survey can pin their diagnostics without\ncounting them as valid requirements. A bare value is not a nominal object, and a name is\nnot a sort merely because it is a declaration:\n\n\n\n4. Census. The valid requirements are surveyed, every produced model port has a\ngoverning requirement, and the clean command enforces that result. The census infers a\nresult port's object domain from its member's type: R3 covers a P-only result, but leaves Q\nungoverned on a generic pair boundary. An unresolved bearer is an unchecked violation unless\nexempted. A .sort scope needs an indexed result whose object type has a Sorted instance:\nR1 and R2's results are borne by the pair whole, and the instance above maps the whole to\npairS and each part to its own sort, so the census checks their scope. An object of another\nsort is reported ungoverned. A .decided scope is checked against a deciderProof theorem\nproving its decider returns true for every result of the governed member; without that\nproof the census reports the port unchecked.\n\n\n\nAll three requirements are discharged: R1 has a theorem and a spot check, R2 has an\nabsent aggregation license, and R3 has a referent-backed gate. The invalid scopes remain\nvisible as counterexamples rather than silently entering the requirement census.\n\nThe sibling-namespace probe shows the scope gap directly: a P-scoped requirement governs\nP's result while the generic pair boundary leaves Q ungoverned.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L6 — What must hold","header":"5.7.7. Lesson","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L6-___-What-must-hold/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L6-___-What-must-hold--Lesson"},"/Uncertainty-quantification-and-numerical-adequacy/Why-formalize-this___-What-the-rigor-buys/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Uncertainty-quantification-and-numerical-adequacy--Why-formalize-this___-What-the-rigor-buys":{"contents":"Uncertainty analysis is routinely done in a spreadsheet or a NumPy script, so casting it in a proof\nassistant earns its keep only by buying something those tools cannot. It buys five things — each\nexercised by a checked example below, not asserted here.\n\n* The published numbers are re-derived under CI, not transcribed. Each worked example reproduces a\n  headline figure of its source paper as a #guard, so the UncertaintyExamples library building\n  is what certifies the figure. Degenhardt's fictive model (UncertaintyExamples.DegenhardtFictive,\n  its GUM answer cross-checked against a Monte Carlo reference) and Willink's\n  gauge block (UncertaintyExamples.WillinkGaugeBlock, by the cumulants\n  method) reproduce their papers' , excess , and expanded-uncertainty half-widths\n  to the printed digits.* The method hierarchy is a theorem, so the three methods cross-validate for free. GUM ⊂ Willink ⊂\n  SSPRC is a chain of proved homomorphisms (T2,\n  T3, resting on T5), not a\n  slogan. Wherever their hypotheses hold the three answers must agree; a discrepancy is a modelling\n  error or a broken proof, caught mechanically rather than by eye\n  (UncertaintyExamples.SsprcNesting pins the shared cumulants ).* The sensitivity coefficients are proved equal to the derivative, not merely computed. The\n   the two linearized rungs need come from one reverse pass over the\n  write-once model (the sensitivity bridge); since Stage 3.5 that\n  reverse pass is proved to be the adjoint of the Fréchet derivative, an endpoint that\n  holds on the very tape the bridge builds. A  entering a\n  budget is therefore correct by theorem, not by trust in an autograd library\n  (UncertaintyExamples.DegenhardtSensitivity returns  and reproduces ).* \"No information loss\" is decided, not hoped for — and at the scale that matters. The hazard this\n  half of the chapter guards is silent: a small-but-significant input uncertainty rounded away in\n  floating point, corrupting the result with no error raised. The adequacy\n  carrier detects exactly that, and its verdict is sound and\n  complete — the flag fires iff a contribution is genuinely lost — at the half-ulp scale the same\n  descriptor defines. UncertaintyExamples.AdequacySwamping flags a contribution swamped under a\n   accumulator and certifies the small-accumulator variant clean, while\n  UncertaintyExamples.AdequacyCoupling drives the significance\n  coupling — the uncertainty budget and the adequacy check from one descriptor list — so a\n  UQ-analysed model is simultaneously adequacy-checked at its own uncertainty scale.* One model serves proof, differentiation, execution, and certification with no re-implementation.\n  All of the above are interpretations of a single write-once kernel at different carriers, so the\n  proved model and the model that runs cannot drift apart — they are one source. The next section\n  makes this composition precise; the working consequence is that an edit to the model is proved,\n  differentiated, run, and adequacy-checked from the one definition.\n\nNone of this substitutes for measurement, or for judgement about which distribution an input follows.\nWhat it removes is a specific class of silent errors:\n\n* a mis-stated propagation law — the combine rules are derived, not asserted: GUM and Willink are\n  proved projections of the finer method (T2,\n  T3), so a wrong law surfaces as a broken proof or a numerical\n  cross-validation mismatch rather than a plausible-looking answer;* a sensitivity taken on trust — the coefficients  are proved equal to the adjoint of the\n  Fréchet derivative on the very tape the sensitivity bridge builds\n  (eager provenance), not read off an unverified autograd library;* an uncertainty contribution quietly lost to rounding — the adequacy\n  carrier detects absorption, and its verdict is\n  sound and complete at the half-ulp scale the same descriptor\n  defines, so a swamped contribution is reported, not dropped in silence.\n\nThe uncertainty formalism turns each into an\nobligation the build must discharge.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Uncertainty quantification and numerical adequacy","header":"19.1. Why formalize this? What the rigor buys","id":"/Uncertainty-quantification-and-numerical-adequacy/Why-formalize-this___-What-the-rigor-buys/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Uncertainty-quantification-and-numerical-adequacy--Why-formalize-this___-What-the-rigor-buys"}});